<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # <span style="color: #000;">Look at the Fireworks!</span> – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Festival Season.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Look at the Fireworks!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify numbers and quantities when heard slowly and clearly. Interactive: Follow directions in a game as instructed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify numbers and quantities when heard in festival contexts (e.g., "three cups of shaved ice"). Can follow directions to perform a simple game (e.g., "Toss the ball!"). Can recognize and repeat numbers related to festival items (e.g., "two balloons").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and sounds.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows colorful flashcards or pictures of festival items (fireworks, balloons, shaved ice, cotton candy, drums, music, party). The teacher says "Look! It's a festival!" and points to a general festival picture. Students listen and point. The teacher plays a short, upbeat festival music clip and encourages students to clap or tap their feet (TPR). The teacher then says "Look at the fireworks!" while pointing to the flashcard. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards and clearly pronounces new vocabulary: "festival", "fireworks" (/f/ sound), "party" (/p/ sound), "balloons", "shaved ice", "cotton candy", "music" (/m/ sound), "drums". The teacher uses simple gestures (e.g., hands up for fireworks, round motion for balloons). The teacher models short sentences: "It is fun!", "I like the fireworks. They are bright." The teacher clearly models the pronunciation of /f/ in "fireworks" and /m/ in "music" and the initial sound P /p/ in "party", exaggerating the sounds for clarity. Students listen and repeat chorally. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key words.
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows "fireworks" flashcard and asks, "Is it a cat or fireworks?" (Students point or say "fireworks" / "fireworks!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a picture of "shaved ice" and asks, "Can we eat this?" (Students nod or say "Yes!").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language and content, connected to the scenario "It's Festival Season."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of simple sentences describing a festival scene (e.g., "Look at the big festival! I see two balloons. I hear music. The fireworks are bright! I like cotton candy. It is fun!"). Alternatively, the teacher reads these sentences slowly and clearly, pointing to corresponding large pictures on a board. The material will also include flashcards of numbers 1-5.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner understands simple, concrete information and short, very clear instructions related to familiar topics or immediate needs, often supported by visual aids.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> The teacher plays the audio (or reads). After listening, the teacher asks, "What do you see?" and encourages students to point to "fireworks" or "balloons" on the board.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Listening (Numbers):</b> The teacher plays the audio again, pausing after sentences with numbers. "I see two balloons." The teacher asks, "How many balloons?" (Students hold up two fingers or point to the number 2 flashcard). "I like one shaved ice." (Students hold up one finger or point to the number 1 flashcard).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual Check):</b> The teacher says, "I see three cotton candies." If there are three in the picture, students give a thumbs up; if not, thumbs down. (Ensure picture matches audio for true scenarios, or mismatch for false).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, preparing students for independent use of language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying numbers and festival-related vocabulary when heard slowly and clearly.
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Subject + verb + adjective ("It is bright!"), Present simple ("I like the music."), WH-questions (Teacher: "What do you like?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> festival, fireworks, party, balloons, shaved ice, cotton candy, corn on the cob, music, drums, fun, bright, colorful. Verbs: like, watch, play, dance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Focused listening on /f/ in "fireworks," /m/ in "music," and P /p/ in "party" through repetition.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Listening to descriptions of favorite festival parts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Exposure to "I like Fiestas Patrias" as part of general listening.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Listening (Numbers & Items):</b> The teacher places large pictures of festival items (e.g., three fireworks, two balloons, one cotton candy, four drums) on the board. The teacher says a sentence, "Point to three fireworks!" or "Show me two balloons!" Students listen and point to the correct picture and quantity. The teacher repeats the sentence slowly and clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Listening (Listen and Respond):</b> The teacher calls out a festival item, e.g., "Cotton candy!" Students listen, then repeat the word. The teacher then asks, "What do you like to eat?" (pointing to cotton candy). Students listen and may respond with "Cotton candy!" or point. The teacher also uses sentences like "I like the music. It is fun!" and students listen and point to "music" or give a thumbs up for "fun."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Game: "Listen and Circle!"</b> Each student receives a worksheet with 5-6 festival items, some repeated with different quantities (e.g., one balloon, three balloons, two fireworks, one fireworks). The teacher says, "Circle two balloons!" or "Circle one shaved ice!" Students listen and circle the correct quantity and item. The teacher walks around to observe.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Festival Season."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> A simple TPR-based game called "Festival Directions." This game requires students to listen to multi-step instructions and respond physically, demonstrating their comprehension of numbers, items, and verbs (like 'toss', 'clap', 'point').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The teacher places large flashcards or realia of festival items (balloons, drums, cotton candy, a small ball to represent "toss") around the classroom. Numbers 1-3 are also clearly visible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. The teacher gives a series of simple instructions, integrating numbers, vocabulary, and verbs:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to two balloons!" (Students listen and point to the flashcard showing two balloons).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Toss the ball! One time!" (Students listen and toss a soft ball one time, if available, or mime the action).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Clap for the music! Three times!" (Students listen and clap three times).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the bright fireworks! One time!" (Students listen and point).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Dance for the festival! Two steps!" (Students listen and make two dance steps).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. The teacher ensures to use descriptive sentences like "It is fun!" or "The fireworks are colorful" within the instructions, prompting non-verbal affirmation from students (e.g., thumbs up).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. The teacher asks a few "What do you like?" questions, encouraging students to point to their favorite festival item among the classroom visuals.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check understanding and application.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Draw/Color." Students receive a simple worksheet with outlines of various festival items. The teacher gives instructions like, "Draw three balloons." "Color one bright firework red." "Draw two drums." "Color the party colorful." Students listen and follow the directions. The teacher walks around, observing students' comprehension. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify numbers (1-3) when heard (e.g., draws correct quantity).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify target vocabulary (e.g., draws/colors the correct item).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can follow simple descriptive instructions (e.g., "bright," "colorful").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows active listening (e.g., focuses, attempts task).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a paper with three simple emoji faces (happy, neutral, sad).
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can listen to numbers (1, 2, 3)." (Circle one face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can listen to festival words." (Circle one face).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and planning for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite festival word you heard today? (Students point to a flashcard or picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can you hear "one" and "two" when I say it? (Students show fingers).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in engaging students in listening to festival vocabulary and numbers? The TPR activities and the "Listen and Draw" game seemed effective for Pre-A1.4 learners. Many students could identify numbers 1-3. Some struggled with multi-word instructions like "Color one bright firework red" and needed more repetition. Adjustments for next lesson: Continue with strong visual support and simpler, shorter listening tasks. Integrate more repetition of grammar structures like "It is fun!" and "I like..." with a clear focus on the /f/, /m/, /p/ sounds. Need to ensure clarity of instruction for the youngest learners.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core festival vocabulary and numbers through listening. The next lesson (Reading Comprehension) will use these same vocabulary words in short, illustrated texts, allowing students to visually recognize the words they have just heard, reinforcing their understanding within the "It's Festival Season" theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>